Now there are many web applications try to prevent attackers from accessing restricted files (like /etc/passwd) by sanitizing user input.
A common mistake developers make is writing a filter that searches for ../ and removes it only one time.
So to bypass this filter we use nesting technique of adding one traversal sequence inside other. Eg - "....//"
- The filter scans for ../: It finds ../ right in the middle: .. + ../ + /
- The filter deletes the middle ../: The outer characters collapse together: .. + / = ../

